WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.193 --> 00:00:05.023
It’s quite fitting that Marilyn vos Savant’s
last name in French means “learned”

00:00:05.023 --> 00:00:09.120
Learning came easy to her considering she
had an IQ of 228!

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:16.726
Vos Savant was born in St Louis, Missouri
on August 11, 1946 to immigrants from Germany and Italy.

00:00:16.726 --> 00:00:19.016
Her parents never told her she was exceptional.

00:00:19.016 --> 00:00:22.966
She once said in an interview: “No one really
paid much attention to me, actually.

00:00:22.966 --> 00:00:25.835
As I said, mostly because I was a girl and
I accepted that.”

00:00:25.835 --> 00:00:30.225
But the world would pay attention in 1985
when she topped the Guinness Book of World

00:00:30.225 --> 00:00:33.536
Records list as the smartest person in the
world.

00:00:33.536 --> 00:00:36.256
She was nearly 40 when she shot into the spotlight.

00:00:36.256 --> 00:00:40.646
Parade Magazine wrote a profile on her, and
readers had so many questions for her that

00:00:40.646 --> 00:00:45.496
the magazine offered her a Sunday column,
“Ask Marilyn” - which exists to this day.

00:00:45.496 --> 00:00:49.976
In this column, she ignited one of the fiercest
debates in probability of the 21st century!

00:00:49.976 --> 00:00:54.456
In 1990, a reader asked her the following
question:

00:00:54.456 --> 00:00:58.356
Suppose you’re on a game show, and you’re
given the choice of three doors.

00:00:58.356 --> 00:01:01.776
Behind one door is a car, behind the others,
goats.

00:01:01.776 --> 00:01:06.816
You pick a door, say #1, and the host, who
knows what’s behind the doors, opens another

00:01:06.816 --> 00:01:09.176
door, say #3, which has a goat.

00:01:09.176 --> 00:01:12.256
He says to you, “Do you want to pick door
#2?”

00:01:12.256 --> 00:01:15.746
Is it to your advantage to switch your choice
of doors?

00:01:15.746 --> 00:01:20.146
This is known as the Monty Hall problem, named
after the former host of the game show Let’s

00:01:20.146 --> 00:01:21.415
Make a Deal.

00:01:21.415 --> 00:01:24.796
Is it behind door number 1, door number 2,
or door number 3?

00:01:24.796 --> 00:01:29.415
So, would it be in your interest to switch
from door number 1 to door number 2?

00:01:29.415 --> 00:01:34.096
I’ll give you a few seconds to think about
it.

00:01:34.096 --> 00:01:38.156
Most people assume that both doors are equally
likely to have the prize.

00:01:38.156 --> 00:01:40.226
So they don’t see the benefit of switching.

00:01:40.226 --> 00:01:44.206
However, Vos Savant replied: “Yes; you should
switch.

00:01:44.206 --> 00:01:49.936
The first door has a 1/3 chance of winning,
but the second door has a 2/3 chance.”

00:01:49.936 --> 00:01:54.316
She got so much heat for this response and
couldn’t have imagined the backlash that

00:01:54.316 --> 00:01:55.386
would follow.

00:01:55.386 --> 00:02:00.236
She received thousands of angry letters and
said 90% of them told her she was wrong.

00:02:00.236 --> 00:02:05.286
Scott Smith who has a PhD from the University
of Florida wrote: There is enough mathematical

00:02:05.286 --> 00:02:09.626
illiteracy in this country, and we don’t
need the world’s highest IQ propagating

00:02:09.626 --> 00:02:10.626
more.

00:02:10.626 --> 00:02:11.626
Shame!

00:02:11.626 --> 00:02:15.086
Here’s a letter from Professor Robert Sachs
of George Mason University:

00:02:15.086 --> 00:02:16.086
You blew it!

00:02:16.086 --> 00:02:19.546
As a professional mathematician, I’m very
concerned with the general public’s lack

00:02:19.546 --> 00:02:21.376
of mathematical skills.

00:02:21.376 --> 00:02:25.736
Please help by confessing your error and in
the future being more careful.”

00:02:25.736 --> 00:02:30.635
Don Edwards of Oregon put it this way: Maybe
women look at math problems differently than

00:02:30.635 --> 00:02:31.635
men.

00:02:31.635 --> 00:02:36.025
But actually, these people who sent her some
not-so-nice letters were utterly wrong.

00:02:36.025 --> 00:02:39.826
Switching your door DOES increase your probability
of winning.

00:02:39.826 --> 00:02:45.906
When you first choose door #1, there’s a
1/3 chance that the prize is behind that one.

00:02:45.906 --> 00:02:47.275
The two other doors together have a 2/3 chance
of winning.

00:02:47.275 --> 00:02:51.516
Then the host helps you out by opening up
the door they KNOW is a loser.

00:02:51.516 --> 00:02:52.786
This improves your odds that the prize is
behind door 2.

00:02:52.786 --> 00:02:55.176
Because door 2 must have the rest of the chances.

00:02:55.176 --> 00:03:00.086
It went from having a 1 in 3 chance to a 2
in 3 shot at the prize since the host filtered

00:03:00.086 --> 00:03:03.186
out the bad door, door number 3 for you.

00:03:03.186 --> 00:03:05.366
Switching doubles your odds of winning.

00:03:05.366 --> 00:03:06.756
Or put another way:

00:03:06.756 --> 00:03:11.336
So yah I’ll choose door number 2 and thank
you for the extra 33.3%.

00:03:11.336 --> 00:03:16.386
The outcry against vos Savant was so extreme
that she felt compelled to devote several

00:03:16.386 --> 00:03:18.976
other columns to explaining her logic.

00:03:18.976 --> 00:03:22.266
She noted that the benefits of switching can
be proven if you were to play through the

00:03:22.266 --> 00:03:24.866
six games that exhaust all possibilities.

00:03:24.866 --> 00:03:28.566
This is contingent on the host always opening
a door with a goat.

00:03:28.566 --> 00:03:32.536
Mapping out all the possibilities shows there’s
a higher chance of winning if you switch than

00:03:32.536 --> 00:03:33.536
if you stay.

00:03:33.536 --> 00:03:36.736
It’s easier to understand the problem if
there are many more doors.

00:03:36.736 --> 00:03:39.306
Say you chose 1 door out of 100.

00:03:39.306 --> 00:03:43.626
The host then eliminates 98 doors that they
know don’t have a prize behind them.

00:03:43.626 --> 00:03:47.055
That leaves two doors - the one you chose
and the only other one remaining.

00:03:47.055 --> 00:03:48.895
Do you switch now?

00:03:48.895 --> 00:03:49.895
Absolutely.

00:03:49.895 --> 00:03:54.026
When you first picked, you only had a 1/100
chance of getting the right door.

00:03:54.026 --> 00:03:57.915
The odds of it being behind the other doors
was 99/100.

00:03:57.915 --> 00:04:02.436
The host then filters out the options for
you by eliminating 98 bad doors that they

00:04:02.436 --> 00:04:04.315
know don't have the prize.

00:04:04.315 --> 00:04:09.265
This is to your advantage because it leaves
the remaining door with the rest of the odds,

00:04:09.265 --> 00:04:12.276
a 99/100 chance of having the car.

00:04:12.276 --> 00:04:14.566
Some eventually admitted they were in the
wrong.

00:04:14.566 --> 00:04:19.116
A team at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology
worked on the problem, and afterward, Seth

00:04:19.116 --> 00:04:21.136
Kalson of MIT admitted:

00:04:21.136 --> 00:04:22.535
You are indeed correct.

00:04:22.535 --> 00:04:26.295
"My colleagues at work had a ball with this
problem, and I dare say that most of them,

00:04:26.295 --> 00:04:28.735
including me at first, thought you were wrong!"

00:04:28.735 --> 00:04:30.645
To which she responded: “Thanks, M.I.T.

00:04:30.645 --> 00:04:31.795
I needed that!”

00:04:31.795 --> 00:04:36.376
And that math professor I mentioned earlier
who sent that not so friendly letter?

00:04:36.376 --> 00:04:40.545
Professor Sachs later conceded, writing: “After
removing my foot from my mouth I'm now eating

00:04:40.545 --> 00:04:41.786
humble pie.

00:04:41.786 --> 00:04:45.785
I vowed as penance to answer all the people
who wrote to castigate me.

00:04:45.785 --> 00:04:49.026
It's been an intense professional embarrassment.”

00:04:49.026 --> 00:04:54.165
Our biggest misconception is assuming that
two choices mean a 50-50 chance of something

00:04:54.165 --> 00:04:55.165
happening.

00:04:55.165 --> 00:04:57.785
This makes sense if we don’t have any other
information.

00:04:57.785 --> 00:05:01.306
If I picked two people and asked who would
win a tennis match and you don’t know anything

00:05:01.306 --> 00:05:04.605
about them, you have a 50-50 shot of getting
it right.

00:05:04.605 --> 00:05:09.445
But if I said Player A just took up the sport
yesterday while Player B has won Wimbledon,

00:05:09.445 --> 00:05:11.665
this would likely change your choice.

00:05:11.665 --> 00:05:13.075
Information matters.

00:05:13.075 --> 00:05:15.765
Just like when the game show host KNEW which
door had a goat.

00:05:15.765 --> 00:05:17.626
They weren’t opening up a door randomly.

00:05:17.626 --> 00:05:22.526
The general idea is the more you know, the
more informed decisions you can make.

00:05:22.526 --> 00:05:27.806
Vos Savant once said: “People that we think
are very smart are not necessarily very smart.”

00:05:27.806 --> 00:05:31.876
She explained they’re more likely to be
educated or experienced rather than intelligent.

00:05:31.876 --> 00:05:35.856
What does she think is holding people back
from their intellectual potential?

00:05:35.856 --> 00:05:40.436
She’s been critical of compulsory schooling
because she says students learn passively;

00:05:40.436 --> 00:05:45.316
they sit there and are told what to believe
instead of learning to think independently.

00:05:45.316 --> 00:05:49.325
She went so far as to say: “I would rather
not see compulsory schooling.”

00:05:49.325 --> 00:05:54.515
As for herself, she never graduated from university,
dropping out of Washington University in St.

00:05:54.515 --> 00:06:00.026
Louis after two years to start a career in
investment before following her real passion,

00:06:00.026 --> 00:06:04.315
writing which led to her famous answer to
the problem that stumped the world.

